# Cron Jobs — Introduction & Practical Use

What a cron job actually is, why backend projects need them, and a real working example: automatically deleting unverified doctor applications after 1 hour.

---

## 1. What is a cron job?

A cron job is a task that runs **automatically, on a schedule, without a person or a request triggering it.** No user clicks a button, no API call kicks it off — it just fires on its own, at whatever interval you define (every minute, every night at 2am, every Monday, etc.), for as long as your server process is running.

The name comes from `cron`, a scheduling daemon built into Unix/Linux systems decades ago. `node-cron` is a JavaScript library that recreates that same scheduling behavior inside a Node.js process, so you don't need a separate system-level tool — the schedule lives in your own application code.

---

## 2. A real-life analogy

Think of a cron job like a recurring alarm on your phone, except the alarm doesn't just ring — it actually *does something* when it goes off, with no one around to press snooze or confirm it.

**A closer real-world example:** a gym automatically charges every member's card on the 1st of each month. No staff member manually processes each payment — a scheduled job runs on the 1st, finds everyone due for billing, and charges them. That's exactly the shape of a cron job: "on this schedule, go do this task, unattended."

---

## 3. Why a developer needs this at all

Without cron, your application only does something in response to *someone asking it to* — a user hitting an endpoint, a webhook firing, etc. But plenty of real tasks need to happen **regardless of whether anyone is actively using the app right now**:

- Cleaning up stale/expired data
- Sending reminder emails
- Generating daily/weekly reports
- Syncing data with an external service on a fixed interval
- Expiring things that were only ever meant to be temporary

None of these have a natural "user click" to trigger them — they need to happen on their own, on a timer.

---

## 4. Where developers actually use cron jobs — key problems it solves

| Use case | Problem it solves |
|---|---|
| **Deleting unverified accounts** (your example below) | Prevents your database from filling up with abandoned signups that never completed email verification |
| **Expiring old sessions / tokens** | Cleans up stale auth tokens or password-reset tokens that were never used, without relying on the user to log out |
| **Sending reminder emails** (e.g. "your subscription renews tomorrow") | Doesn't require a user action — the reminder has to go out *before* something happens, on a calendar, not on-demand |
| **Generating scheduled reports** (daily sales summary, weekly analytics digest) | Produces the report once, on schedule, instead of recalculating it live every time someone requests it |
| **Retrying failed background jobs** (e.g. a failed payment webhook) | Gives failed operations another chance without a person noticing and manually retrying |
| **Database backups** | Needs to happen on a fixed schedule regardless of traffic or usage |
| **Cache invalidation / refresh** | Keeps cached data from going stale indefinitely when nothing else would naturally refresh it |
| **Deactivating trial accounts after N days** | Enforces a business rule (trial period ends) without needing the user to trigger anything |

**The common thread:** cron jobs solve the problem of *"this needs to happen based on time passing, not based on a user action."* Anything phrased as "after X hours/days, do Y" is almost always a cron job candidate.

